-- ...and the `DROP DEFAULT` spelling of the same, which completes the
-- (`ALWAYS` | `BY DEFAULT`) x (`SET` | `DROP`) matrix.
--
-- What the four cells are worth, measured by mutation rather than argued. Deleting the
-- gate reddens at least five: all four, plus
-- `set_default_on_identity_beats_missing_sequence.sql`. Each axis then needs a cell on
-- its far side — but **neither mistake is
-- confined to one cell**, so "either mistake still passes the three it does not touch"
-- was simply false: a gate keyed on the **generation**
-- (`Some(IdentityGeneration::Always)`) reddens *both* `BY DEFAULT` cells and neither
-- `ALWAYS` one, and a gate keyed on the statement's **new default**
-- (`column.identity.is_some() && default.is_some()`, which skips `DROP DEFAULT`'s
-- absent value) reddens *both* `DROP` cells and neither `SET` one. The 2x2 is the
-- complete cross, not four independent single-cell probes; {`ALWAYS` x `SET`,
-- `BY DEFAULT` x `DROP`} alone would catch all three of those mutations.
--
-- "The default expression" names a third mutation the matrix cannot see at all, which
-- is why the phrase had to go: a gate keyed on the column's **existing** default
-- (`matches!(column.default, Some(ColumnDefault::NextVal(_)))`) passes all four cells,
-- because in pglet's model an identity column carries exactly that default. Its one red
-- is `ddl/alter_column_drop_default_nextval_plain_column.sql` — a *plain* column with a
-- nextval default, which must still accept `DROP DEFAULT`. That complement belongs to
-- this guard set even though it is not an identity fixture.
-- @setup
CREATE TABLE ddbd_t (id INT GENERATED BY DEFAULT AS IDENTITY, b INT);
-- @test
ALTER TABLE ddbd_t ALTER COLUMN id DROP DEFAULT;
-- @expect-error: column is an identity column (42601)
